## Retro

Two layers of drift in this spec:

1. **Architectural-shift drift (current):** Folder rename and new
   consumer (CLI) should have triggered a `/aide:spec` pass. The
   "init orchestrator" / "calling agent" terminology hardcodes the
   MCP-tool consumer.

2. **Prior cycle (resolved):** The seed-semantic / canonical-template
   idempotency distinction was a deliberate design pivot that landed
   cleanly. The spec's Strategy section codified the two-idempotency-mode
   distinction explicitly and named the distinguishing question ("who
   owns the bytes after first write?"). The parameterized regression
   test (`describe.each` over four on-disk states) is the right
   regression anchor for a data-loss-class invariant.

Stage to watch going forward: the prior cycle introduced placeholder
wikilinks (`[[your-conventions-note]]`, `[[your-folder-structure-note]]`)
and a negative assertion in the test. Any future edit to
`PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` that adds named wikilinks (even well-intentioned
ones) will trip the negative assertion. The spec's
`outcomes.undesired[1]` is the first place a future implementor should
look when that assertion fails.
